@meta
id: 06-09
title: Stopping just short with t
chapter: 06
type: lesson
skills: find-t
par: 24
minutes: 4
vscode: click in the gap before the closing brace
open: payment.service.ts

@brief
The refund call posts only the order id. Support cannot tell refunds apart
in the payment provider's dashboard, so the reason goes in the body too:

    { orderId, reason }

The gap you are typing into is between `orderId` and the space that precedes
the closing brace. Aim at the brace — it is the only one on the line — but
you need to arrive one character short of it, not on it. Land on the brace
itself and your text ends up on the wrong side of the space.

@keys
| `f}` | forward onto the next `}` |
| `t}` | forward until just before the next `}` |
| `i` | insert to the left of the cursor |

@hints
`f}` puts you on the brace, and inserting there would give you
`{ orderId , reason}`. You want to stop one character earlier.
--
`t` is `f` that stops short: `t}` leaves the cursor on the character
immediately before the brace, which here is the space.
--
`2j` to the post call, then `t}`, `i`, type `, reason`, `Esc`.

@solution
    2j t } i , reason <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Think of it as touch versus reach. `f}` touches the brace. `t}` reaches up
to it and stops — the `t` is for till.

The one-character difference decides which side of the surrounding
whitespace your text lands on, and in formatted code that is the difference
between a clean diff and a prettier complaint. Here `t}i` writes into the
gap after `orderId`; `f}i` would write into the gap before the brace.

`T` does the same thing backwards, which is the next lesson. And from
chapter 8 the pair earns its real keep with operators — `dt,` deletes up to
but not including the next comma, which is how you cut one argument out of a
call without touching the punctuation around it.

@start payment.service.ts
export class PaymentService {
  public async refund(orderId: string, reason: string) {
    return this.http.post('/refunds', { orderId });
  }
}

@expected payment.service.ts
export class PaymentService {
  public async refund(orderId: string, reason: string) {
    return this.http.post('/refunds', { orderId, reason });
  }
}
